== SUMMARY (13:40)
Where the nodes go (prof.cpp stats, engine's old rule): ~97% of nodes lead to no tiling; ~90% sit on the last vertex level and
97% of those are dead on arrival (some free half-edge has no partner). Isomorphic partial states (transposition table / DP
over whole states) cover only 2.5-7% of nodes, and with the full-tiling key no exact state ever repeated; leaves are 1% of
nodes, 89% of them covers of smaller tilings, multiplicity among minimal ones ~1.5. So isomorph rejection is not the lever.
Lever 1, fail first (MRV + forward checking): branch on the free half-edge with the fewest options, die when one has none.
  A6_30 k<=6: 66.9M -> 1.6M nodes. Loses 1.4-1.6x on squares_edges and r4 (few dead ends): a pilot to 2 vertices picks per board.
Lever 2, exact option counts cached by local context (DP over contexts): what a fresh vertex can glue to depends only on the
  partial faces around the half-edge; signatures make a miss one face test per distinct template neighbourhood.
  A6_30 k<=6: 1.6M -> 0.33M nodes, CPU 3.8 -> 1.6 s; 1247 k<=5: 8.2M -> 1.0M nodes, CPU 14.3 -> 9.9 s; cold cache costs up to ~1 s.
Not worth it (measured): one-vertex budget cover bound (2-5%), lazy exact counting (no change), tie-break by face rule.
Correctness: verify.py on 10 boards x 3 rule modes, every output file's count equal to the committed engine, tileestimate
  --exact equal to the engine, stop/resume equal: 90/90. Context key validated at every hit (prof VALIDATE=1, 6 boards);
  signatures validated against every placement's own test (-DCHECK_PASSING, 9 boards).
Estimates for the A6_30 draft board, new engine: k<=12 0.34-0.41 core-hours, k<=15 7-11, k<=20 ~3.3e11 nodes,
  600-1,600 core-hours (seeds agree on nodes within 4%). Real runs: k<=8 2.66M nodes (est 2.4-2.5M), k<=10 21.9M (est
  16.6-18.3M): the estimator reads 5-25% low at depth. Committed engine, k<=20 estimate: 1.9e16-2.3e18 nodes.
